/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Same Tree
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Binary Tree
Difficulty : Easy

Problem:
Given the roots of two binary trees p and q, determine whether
the two trees are the same.

Two binary trees are considered the same if:
- They have the same structure.
- Corresponding nodes contain the same values.

Example:

    p = [1,2,3]
    q = [1,2,3]

Both trees have the same structure and node values.

Output:

    true

Approach:
Use Recursion / Depth First Search (DFS).

For every pair of corresponding nodes, check three conditions:

1. Both nodes are nullptr.
   -> The corresponding subtrees are empty, so they are same.

2. Only one node is nullptr.
   -> The structures are different, so return false.

3. Both nodes exist but their values are different.
   -> The trees are different, so return false.

If all three checks pass, recursively compare:
- Left subtree of p with left subtree of q.
- Right subtree of p with right subtree of q.

Therefore:

    same tree =
        same left subtree
        AND
        same right subtree

Example:

    p:              q:

        1               1
       / \             / \
      2   3           2   3

Compare roots:

    1 == 1

Compare left subtrees:

    2 == 2

Compare right subtrees:

    3 == 3

All corresponding nodes match, so return true.

Example of different structure:

    p:              q:

        1               1
       /                 \
      2                   2

Root values are equal, but:

    p->left  exists
    q->left  is nullptr

Therefore, the trees are not the same.

Why do we compare both left and right subtrees?

A binary tree's identity depends on both:
- Node values
- Tree structure

So matching only the values is not enough.

Algorithm:
1. If both nodes are nullptr, return true.
2. If one of the nodes is nullptr, return false.
3. If their values are different, return false.
4. Recursively compare the left subtrees.
5. Recursively compare the right subtrees.
6. Return true only if both subtree comparisons are true.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
In the worst case, every node of both trees is visited once.

More precisely, the complexity depends on the number of
corresponding nodes that need to be checked.

Space Complexity : O(h)

Reason:
The recursion stack depends on the height of the tree.

For a balanced tree:

    O(log n)

For a skewed tree:

    O(n)

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:

    bool isSameTree(TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {

        // Both nodes are empty
        if (p == nullptr && q == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        // Only one node is empty
        if (p == nullptr || q == nullptr) {
            return false;
        }

        // Values are different
        if (p->val != q->val) {
            return false;
        }

        // Compare both subtrees
        return isSameTree(p->left, q->left) &&
               isSameTree(p->right, q->right);
    }
};
